!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/api-2.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  A description of an HTTP API whose payloads are TSON documents. A DATA document governed by this
  schema, not a schema of its own -- so a description is written, published and versioned like any
  other document, and needs nothing of the type system that is not already there.

  WHY THE TYPE NAMES ARE BARE, AND WHOSE JOB IT IS TO CHECK THEM.

  A data document cannot hold a reference to a type. Type-name resolution happens at type-ref
  positions in a SCHEMA document and nowhere else, so a name written here is an inert token as far
  as the resolver is concerned -- see SCHEMA-HEADER.md and sketch/README.md for the long form. That
  is not a defect to design around; it is the layering.

  So this document carries its own import list and its own namespace rule, and TsonApi resolves type
  names against it -- the same shape as a schema's !!import, one layer down and enforced by a
  consumer instead of by the resolver:

    - `imports` names the schemas whose types this API may reference.
    - A type name is bare and is resolved against those imports, in the order given.
    - A name declared by two different imports is ambiguous, and is an error naming both.
    - A name no import declares is an error naming what the imports do declare.

  api-1.tn spelled every reference as { schema: uri  type: text } -- the pair repeated at every
  payload, and never checked. This carries the schema list once and checks the names. What it cannot
  do is have the resolver check them, which is what sketch/ is about.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  parameter_location => !enum [PATH QUERY HEADER]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    A type this API references, named bare and resolved against the document's own `imports`.

    Not a (schema, type) pair: carrying the schema at every use site is what api-1.tn did, and it
    repeats information the import list already holds while still checking nothing.
    """
  type_name => !text ^ { min_length: 1 }

  @doc:"""
    A parameter carried outside the body. `type` names a scalar -- a URL segment cannot carry a
    record -- which TsonApi checks along with everything else, since nothing else will.
    """
  parameter => {
    name:        type_name
    in:          parameter_location
    type:        type_name
    required:    boolean
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"`body` is absent for a response that carries none: a 204, or a document served as bytes."
  response => {
    status:      status_code
    body:        type_name?
    description: text?
  }

  @doc:"""
    `path` is a template: a braced segment names a PATH parameter, as in /orders/{id}. A server may
    route however it likes; this states the shape a client should expect.
    """
  operation => {
    method:      http_method
    path:        text
    summary:     text?
    parameters:  [parameter]
    request:     type_name?
    responses:   [response]
  }

  @doc:"""
    `imports` is this document's own namespace, and the reason type names can be bare. It is a list
    of schema identities, resolved in order, exactly as a schema's own !!import list is.

    `base` is where the API is served, absent when that is not fixed -- a description is about a
    contract, not a deployment.
    """
  api => {
    title:      text
    version:    text
    base:       uri?
    imports:    [uri]
    operations: [operation]
  }
}
